/*
Problem: Minimum Common Value (LC 2540)
Topic: Two Pointers

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

Both arrays are already sorted.

Use two pointers to traverse them
simultaneously.

• If both elements are equal,
  we have found the smallest common
  value.

• If one element is smaller,
  move that pointer forward because
  it cannot match any previous value
  in the other array.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

1. Start pointers at index 0 of
   both arrays.

2. Compare current elements.

3. If equal,
   return the value.

4. If nums1[i] < nums2[j],
   move i.

5. Otherwise,
   move j.

6. If no common value exists,
   return -1.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

nums1 = [1,2,3,6]

nums2 = [2,3,4,5]

Compare:

1 < 2

Move i

2 == 2

Answer = 2

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

nums1 = [1,2,4]

nums2 = [3,5,6]

1 < 3 → move i

2 < 3 → move i

4 > 3 → move j

4 < 5 → move i

No common value.

Answer = -1

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

Since both arrays are sorted,

if one value is smaller than the
other, it can never become equal
by staying in place.

So we safely move the pointer with
the smaller value.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n + m)

Each pointer moves at most once
through its array.

Space : O(1)

Only two pointers are used.

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Two Pointers on Sorted Arrays
*/

class Solution {
public:
    int getCommon(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2) {

        int i = 0;
        int j = 0;

        while (i < nums1.size() &&
               j < nums2.size()) {

            if (nums1[i] == nums2[j])
                return nums1[i];

            else if (nums1[i] < nums2[j])
                i++;

            else
                j++;
        }

        return -1;
    }
};
